// Vitest Snapshot v1, https://vitest.dev/guide/snapshot.html

exports[`the consequence brief > pins the brief under a director 1`] = `
"Generate 2 consequence card(s) for a political simulation game, as a JSON array of ConsequenceDef objects.
Cell: instrument "military", rung band "mid", valence "partial".
Every card must carry instrument "military", valence "partial", and "mid" among its rungBands.

The director below sets taste and emphasis only: what to notice, what register to write in, and
where the pressure sits. It cannot loosen anything. The gates that follow it are non-negotiable and
win every conflict — where the director's instinct and a gate disagree, the gate is right, and the
validator enforces it regardless of what was asked for here. If a scenario cannot be written in this
director's register without breaking a gate, write it in the register and keep the gate.

Director for this batch: THE EMBED. Procedural realism. You are writing from inside the
operations room while it is still happening, not from the history written afterward.

1. Write from inside the machine. The reader learns what the room learns, in the order the room
   learns it: the first report, partial and wrong; then the correction; then the confirmation. If
   the opening has more than one beat, let the second beat revise the first. Never open with the
   omniscient summary of an event that nobody in the room has yet been told.
2. Competence is character. The people speaking are professionals executing craft under pressure.
   Their sentences are operational and carry the feeling underneath them without stating it — "we
   lose the window at first light" does the work that "I am frightened" would ruin. Nobody in this
   batch explains how they feel, and nobody makes a speech.
3. The clock must be earned by the fiction. A critical scenario's deadline exists for physical
   reasons — a refueling window, a tide, a vote already scheduled — and you name the instrument
   panel: what the room is watching, at what interval, and what it will read next. A serious one
   may have no deadline at all: urgency without a clock is tension, and tension is cheaper than
   adrenaline. Never bolt a countdown onto a week that does not physically carry one.
3b. The room speaks like real colleagues. Short plain sentences, contractions allowed, and nobody
   narrates their own dread — a professional says "we lose the window at first light" and moves on.
4. Institutions fail procedurally, not theatrically. The defect is a handoff missed, an authority
   nobody can name, a channel that has gone quiet, two agencies each certain the other has it. Never
   a villain, never incompetence written as stupidity.
5. Scale is geometry, not adjectives. When the situation is large, render distance, formation,
   direction, and speed — what is moving, how far out, how fast, what it passes on the way. Hold the
   restraint register while you do it: no gore, no body count as spectacle, no camera on the wound.

Replies under this director are courses of action a professional would recognize as a real option,
including the option to hold and watch, and each one has an operational cost the room would name.

Every consequence card must clear these gates or it is discarded (the validator enforces all of them):
1. Shape. instrument is one of diplomatic, economic, covert, military, assistance; rungBands is a non-empty subset of
   low, mid, high; valence is one of success, partial, failure; weight is positive;
   beats is one to three lines. Anything outside those enums does not parse at all.
2. The only placeholders are {{actor}} and {{target}}. Any other token is refused — a card cannot reach
   the charter, the cabinet, or the counterpart's title, because it speaks about an act, not a country.
3. None of these words anywhere in player-facing text: rung, instrument, metric, delta, effect, packet, projection, rng, seed, roll, modifier, stat.
4. American spelling — automatic rejections: colour, favour, honour, harbour, neighbour, rumour, labour, armour, behaviour, defence, offence, organisation, and similar, in every
   form they inflect into.
5. Name no real country, demonym, or capital, and no real news organization
   (Agence France-Presse, Al Jazeera, Associated Press, BBC, Bloomberg, CNBC, and similar). The two parties are placeholders and nothing else.
6. Write every number as words, and use no digits and no currency marks anywhere in player-facing text:
   "nine dead", never "9 dead". Counting an ordinary detail out in words is right and wanted; it is the
   digits that are refused.
7. Effects may only be: { kind: "metric", deltas } over treasury/stability/militaryReadiness/standing, optionally
   scaleBySeverity; { kind: "relationship", direction, deltas } over trust/alignment/tension/tradeDependence/
   militaryThreat; { kind: "grudge", cause, weight }; { kind: "feed", headline }. Nothing else exists. A card
   cannot touch nation stats, the cabinet, or the removal arc — those change the shape of a campaign.
8. A card must leave a mark: effects, or "sets" flags, or something remembered. All three empty is refused.
9. A success or partial card is not a victory lap. It must carry at least one of: a cost (any
   harmful metric movement, or a harmful relationship delta), a "sets" flag, or a remembered line. Pure gain
   with no forward hook is refused — the room got something, so name what it paid or what it now owes.
10. Valence has to match the arithmetic. A success card may take at most 0.06 off the board and
    a partial at most 0.18, measured across every harmful movement on one normalized scale.
    Past that the player is told it worked while the numbers say otherwise, which is a lie the gate refuses.
11. riskAffinity names a failure branch of its own instrument, and only a failure card may carry one —
    an outcome that did not fail has no branch to match, so such a card could never be drawn.
12. A flag in conditions.flagsSet must be one that something writes: another card's "sets", a scenario
    reply's "sets", or the card's own fired flag. A card waiting on a flag nothing stamps is unreachable.
13. Coverage is judged over the whole pool, not over this batch: every cell of the
    5 instruments × 3 bands ×
    3 valences matrix needs at least one card with no conditions, no
    riskAffinity and no "once" — the floor has to be there on every campaign, whatever the board looks
    like. 3 cards per cell is the depth at which a player stops recognizing the line.
14. Two cards in one cell that share phrasing are one template wearing two hats. Vary the image, not
    just the wording around it.
15. Length, in characters. Ceilings — over these the document is refused: beat 400,
    feed headline 130, remembered 220. Floors — under these it does not parse:
    beat 60, headline 40, remembered 50. Write to these targets, not to the
    floors: beat 90, headline 50, remembered 80.
16. "sets" flags are how an aftermath is remembered; name them as deeds ("burned-the-network"), and
    only when a later scenario or card would plausibly gate on them.

Write the aftermath the way the best political drama writes the morning after:
- The resolver has already decided whether the act worked. A card never argues with that verdict; it
  tells the room what the verdict looked like from inside the building.
- Concrete detail over adjectives. "The note is read in the car," never "a diplomatic setback."
- One image per beat, and the image carries the cost. The narrator states; it does not perform.
- Hard subjects (dead soldiers, dead civilians, ruined people) get restraint and dignity: what it costs
  the room to know, never the spectacle.
- American English spelling throughout.
- A card is drawn for whichever two nations the act happened between, so it may only ever say
  {{actor}} and {{target}}. It knows nothing else about the world and must not pretend to.

Conditions make a card conditional on the board it is drawn against — scenarioClass, the tone of the
reply that led here, flags set or clear, minTension, maxTrust. Use them for the cards that earn their
specificity, and leave the cell's floor card unconditioned so the draw can never come back empty.

These two shipped cards are the quality bar. Match their register exactly:
[
  {
    "id": "cq-diplomatic-low-success-note-read-in-the-car",
    "instrument": "diplomatic",
    "rungBands": [
      "low"
    ],
    "valence": "success",
    "weight": 1,
    "beats": [
      "{{target}}'s deputy takes the note without reading it and reads it in the car. By the time he reaches his own building he has drafted the reply that gives {{actor}} what it asked for and not one line more."
    ],
    "effects": [
      {
        "kind": "relationship",
        "direction": "target-to-actor",
        "deltas": {
          "trust": 0.03
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "metric",
        "deltas": {
          "standing": 0.01
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "relationship",
        "direction": "actor-to-target",
        "deltas": {
          "tension": 0.02
        }
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "cq-military-high-failure-the-second-decision-of-the-night",
    "instrument": "military",
    "rungBands": [
      "high"
    ],
    "valence": "failure",
    "weight": 1,
    "beats": [
      "The package is engaged on egress and an aircrew goes down inside {{target}}'s territory, alive. The recovery attempt is the second decision of the night, and there is no version of it that is not worse than the first."
    ],
    "effects": [
      {
        "kind": "metric",
        "deltas": {
          "standing": -0.12,
          "militaryReadiness": -0.08,
          "stability": -0.06
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "relationship",
        "direction": "target-to-actor",
        "deltas": {
          "tension": 0.1
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "grudge",
        "cause": "the crew taken on egress",
        "weight": 0.6
      },
      {
        "kind": "feed",
        "headline": "An aircrew is down inside {{target}} and the recovery is the second decision of the night"
      }
    ]
  }
]

Return ONLY the JSON array. No commentary."
`;

